# SYNOPSIS

```usage
rm [-f] [-iRr] file ...
```

# DESCRIPTION

`rm` removes each <file>.
A directory is refused unless `-r` is given.
A symbolic link is removed, not what it leads to.
When standard input is a terminal, `rm` is meant to ask before removing a <file> it may not write, unless `-f` is given (see BUGS).

: `-f`
  Ask nothing and report nothing: a <file> that does not exist, or that cannot be removed, is passed over without a word.
  With no <file> at all, `rm -f` does nothing and succeeds.
: `-i`
  Ask about every file before removing it, as `remove file '`<file>`'? `; an answer starting with `y` or `Y`, read from standard input, removes it.
: `-R`, `-r`
  Remove directories and what they hold, deepest first.
  Symbolic links met on the way are removed, never followed.

A <file> whose last component is `.` or `..`, or that is the root directory, is refused, and the others are still removed.

`rm` is sbase's, built into `sbase-box` with sbase's other commands and installed as `/boot/bin/posix/rm`, a hard link to it.
In the POSIX namespace (a manifest's `ns=posix`, made by `/lib/ns/posix`, see namespace(6)) `/boot/bin/posix` is bound before `/boot/bin` on `/bin`, so POSIX programs find it as `/bin/rm`.
There is no native `rm`.
Files are removed with 9P's `Tremove` (see remove(5)).

# EXAMPLES

`tests/posix/sbasetest.c` runs `rm -r /tmp/sb` in the POSIX namespace on a tree of files, a directory and a symbolic link in tmpfs(4), and checks that it prints nothing, exits 0 and leaves no `/tmp/sb`.

# SEE ALSO

rmdir(1), unlink(1), remove(5), namespace(6); sbase's page is `third_party/sbase/rm.1` in the repository

# DIAGNOSTICS

Messages go to standard error, after the name `rm` was run by: `rm: cannot remove '`<file>`':` and the error, `rm: stat` <file>`:` and the error, `"." and ".." may not be removed` and `"/" may not be removed`, each said once.
With no <file> and no `-f`, or an unknown flag, `rm` prints its usage message.
The exit status is 1 if any <file> was refused or not removed, otherwise 0.

# BUGS

`-f` hides every failure, not only a <file> that does not exist as POSIX has it: a file that cannot be removed is kept, and the exit status is still 0.

When both `-f` and `-i` are given `rm` never asks, whichever came last; POSIX has the last one win.

With `-i` and `-r`, a directory named as a <file> is asked about only after what it holds has been asked about and removed; a directory inside it is asked about first.
The question says `file` for a directory too.

On VectraOS `rm` never asks without `-i`: the C library's `faccessat` checks only that the file exists, leaving permissions to the server, so every file seems writable.
